::STAT_U03_L06_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
What type of variables are summarized in a two-way frequency table?
{
~A. One numerical variable only#That would be univariate numerical data.
~B. One equation only#Equations are not frequency tables.
=C. Two categorical variables#Two-way frequency tables summarize bivariate categorical data.
~D. A residual and a prediction#Those belong to model fit, not categorical tables.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
What is bivariate categorical data?
{
~A. One category measured once#Bivariate means two variables.
~B. Two numerical predictions#Those are numerical, not categorical.
=C. Two categorical variables measured for each case#Bivariate categorical data involve two category variables.
~D. A single average#An average summarizes numerical data.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
In a two-way table, a count inside the table is called what?
{
~A. Marginal frequency#Marginal frequencies are row or column totals.
~B. Grand total#The grand total is the total number of cases.
~C. Correlation coefficient#That measures linear association for numerical data.
=D. Joint frequency#Inside cells combine one row category and one column category.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
In a two-way table, a row total is called what?
{
~A. Joint frequency#A joint frequency is an inside cell.
~B. Correlation#Correlation is for numerical variables.
~C. Residual#Residuals compare actual and predicted values.
=D. Marginal frequency#Row and column totals are marginal frequencies.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
A row has counts 7 and 13. What is the row total?
{
=A. 20#7 + 13 = 20.
~B. 6#This subtracts the counts.
~C. 13#This is one count, not the total.
~D. 91#This multiplies the counts.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_Q06::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
A row has counts 16 and 24. What is the row total?
{
~A. 8#This subtracts the counts.
~B. 24#This is one cell, not the total.
=C. 40#16 + 24 = 40.
~D. 384#This multiplies the counts.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_Q07::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
A column has counts 11 and 19. What is the column total?
{
=A. 30#11 + 19 = 30.
~B. 8#This subtracts the counts.
~C. 19#This is one cell, not the total.
~D. 209#This multiplies the counts.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_Q08::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
A column has counts 25 and 15. What is the column total?
{
~A. 10#This subtracts the counts.
~B. 25#This is one cell, not the total.
=C. 40#25 + 15 = 40.
~D. 375#This multiplies the counts.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_Q09::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
What is the grand total in a two-way table?
{
~A. One inside cell#That is a joint frequency.
=B. The total number of cases in the table#The grand total sums all cases.
~C. One category label#Labels are not totals.
~D. The correlation coefficient#Correlation coefficient is for numerical data.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_Q10::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
If row totals are 35 and 45, what is the grand total?
{
~A. 10#This subtracts the row totals.
=B. 80#35 + 45 = 80.
~C. 45#This is only one row total.
~D. 1575#This multiplies the row totals.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_Q11::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
Which statement correctly interprets an inside cell of 18 in a table of grade level by study method?
{
~A. There are 18 students total in the whole survey#An inside cell is not necessarily the grand total.
~B. There are 18 categories#The number is a count of cases, not categories.
=C. 18 students fall into one grade level and one study method category#An inside cell is a joint frequency.
~D. The correlation coefficient is 18#Correlation coefficients are between -1 and 1.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_Q12::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
Which statement correctly interprets a row total of 42?
{
=A. 42 cases are in that row category#A row total counts all cases in that row category.
~B. 42 is one joint frequency only#A row total combines cells in a row.
~C. 42 is the slope of the table#Tables do not have slopes.
~D. 42 proves causation#Counts do not prove causation.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_Q13::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
What may suggest association in a two-way table?
{
~A. All row totals being written at the edge#That is normal table structure, not association by itself.
~B. A table title#A title does not show association.
~C. Every count being positive#Positive counts alone do not show association.
=D. Different distributions across categories#Association may appear when one variable's distribution changes across categories of another variable.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_Q14::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
What should you avoid claiming from a two-way frequency table alone?
{
~A. The variables may be associated#This can be a careful conclusion if supported by the counts.
~B. A row total is a marginal frequency#This is accurate vocabulary.
=C. One category caused the other#A table alone does not prove cause and effect.
~D. An inside cell is a joint frequency#This is accurate vocabulary.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_Q15::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
Which item is a marginal frequency?
{
=A. A column total#Column totals are marginal frequencies.
~B. A count inside the table#That is a joint frequency.
~C. A row label#A label names a category.
~D. A scatterplot point#Scatterplots are for numerical data.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_Q16::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
Which item is a joint frequency?
{
~A. A row total#That is marginal frequency.
~B. A column total#That is marginal frequency.
~C. The grand total#The grand total is all cases.
=D. A count inside the table#Joint frequencies are inside cells.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_Q17::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
A two-way table compares pet ownership and housing type. What are the variables?
{
=A. Pet ownership and housing type#These are the two categorical variables being compared.
~B. Row total and column total#These are table summaries, not the original variables.
~C. Mean and median#Those summarize numerical data.
~D. Slope and intercept#Those describe linear models.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_Q18::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
A two-way table compares transportation method and grade level. Which is a possible joint category?
{
~A. All students#That is the grand total group, not a joint category.
~B. Transportation total#That is a marginal idea.
~C. Grade level only#A joint category needs both variables.
=D. 10th grade students who ride the bus#This combines one grade category and one transportation category.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_Q19::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
Why are totals important in a two-way table?
{
~A. They prove causation#Totals do not prove causation.
=B. They help check and interpret the table#Row, column, and grand totals help verify and summarize counts.
~C. They remove the need for inside cells#Inside cells are still needed.
~D. They turn categories into numerical variables#Totals summarize counts but do not change variable type.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_Q20::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
If two groups have very different sizes, what should you be careful about?
{
~A. Comparing only raw counts#This can be misleading when group sizes differ.
=B. Comparing raw counts without considering group size#Different group sizes can make raw counts misleading.
~C. Looking at row totals#Row totals help show group size.
~D. Using context#Context is helpful.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_Q21::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
What does a two-way table summarize?
{
=A. Counts for combinations of two categorical variables#It summarizes how cases fall into category combinations.
~B. Only one numerical distribution#That would need a different display.
~C. A line of best fit#That belongs to bivariate numerical modeling.
~D. Exponential growth factors#That belongs to exponential modeling.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_Q22::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
Which conclusion is careful for a two-way table?
{
=A. The table suggests a possible association#A table can suggest association without proving cause.
~B. The table proves one variable caused the other#This overclaims causation.
~C. The table proves all future results#A table summarizes observed data, not all future outcomes.
~D. The table has no context#Tables should be interpreted in context.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_Q23::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
Which pair would be appropriate for a two-way frequency table?
{
~A. Height and weight#These are numerical variables, better suited to a scatterplot.
~B. Time and temperature#These are numerical variables.
~C. Score and study hours#These are numerical variables.
=D. Grade level and club membership#Both are categorical variables.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_Q24::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
What is the best first step when constructing a two-way table?
{
~A. Compute a correlation coefficient#Correlation coefficient is for numerical variables.
=B. Identify the two categorical variables and their categories#The table structure depends on the variables and categories.
~C. Draw a line of fit#Lines of fit are for scatterplots.
~D. Calculate residuals#Residuals are for model fit.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_Q25::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
Why should a table interpretation mention context?
{
~A. Context proves causation#Context helps interpretation but does not prove cause.
=B. Context explains what the categories and counts mean#Counts are meaningful only when tied to the real situation.
~C. Context replaces the table#The table is still needed.
~D. Context makes every association strong#Strength depends on the data, not context alone.
}
